></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">112</span></span></span></span> GB: more than an 80 GB GPU holds, before a single activation is stored. Mixed precision does not shrink this; L14 shows how to split it across GPUs.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/p5",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>You must store both activations as large as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^5</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and the difference between <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.000</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.000</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.001</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.001</span></span></span></span>. Which 16-bit format keeps both?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>fp16.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>bf16.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Neither: fp16 overflows at <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^5</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and bf16 cannot tell 1.000 from 1.001.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>Both.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>fp16's largest value is 65504, so <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^5</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> overflows; bf16's spacing near 1 is about 0.0078, so 1.001 rounds to 1.000. Each 16-bit format gives up one of the two, which is why training recipes keep reductions and master weights in fp32, and why fp8 needs per-tensor scale factors.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept1",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How do bf16 and fp16 differ?",
  "answer": "Both use 16 bits; bf16 keeps fp32's 8 exponent bits (same range, about 3 significant digits) while fp16 has 5 exponent bits and 10 mantissa bits (more precision, but a maximum of 65504 and early underflow).",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept2",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why does mixed-precision training keep fp32 master weights?",
  "answer": "Updates are often smaller than the spacing between half-precision numbers near the weight's value, so applied directly to fp16 weights they round away; an fp32 copy accumulates them.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept3",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What does loss scaling do?",
  "answer": "It multiplies the loss by a large factor before the backward pass so small gradients do not underflow in fp16, then divides the gradients by the same factor before the update; dynamic scaling lowers the factor when gradients overflow.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept4",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "Why is a long sum accumulated in fp32 even when its inputs are fp16?",
  "answer": "Once the running sum is large, its spacing exceeds the small addends, which then round away; tensor cores and well-written kernels accumulate in fp32.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept5",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "What is stochastic rounding and why is it useful?",
  "answer": "Rounding up or down at random with probabilities that make the expected result exact; tiny updates then accumulate on average instead of always rounding away.",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
 {
  "id": "L13/concept6",
  "lesson": "L13",
  "kind": "concept",
  "prompt": "How many bytes per parameter does mixed-precision training with Adam need, without activations?",
  "answer": "About 16: fp16 weights and gradients (2 + 2) plus fp32 master weights and two Adam moments (4 + 4 + 4).",
  "answerKind": "text"
 },
